#
# Turn and decision mechanics — how the engine actually offers and applies orders.
#
# HAND-AUTHORED. Unlike thinker/alphax.ttl this is not generated: there is no data file to
# parse, because these facts are behaviour rather than rows. Edit this file directly; do not
# point an ingester at it.
#
# Why they are in the graph at all: they are the facts a *playing* agent needs and cannot read
# off a world view. "Hurrying spends credits irreversibly" is not in the action space; it is the
# reason an agent should think twice before choosing it. See ../docs/turn-scoped-play.md.
#
# Every node carries the three provenance predicates RuleProvenanceShape requires
# (appliesToEngine, ruleTier, sourcedFrom). Tiers are used strictly:
#   canonical       stock SMAC behaviour, true of any engine playing the game
#   house-rule      a Thinker override or addition
#   engine-observed read out of engine C++; carries smac:computedBy
#
# NOTE on smac:computedBy targets: these name the implementing functions, but those symbols are
# NOT yet Hank-promoted — promotion is gated on Hank Phase 4 (docs/policy-harness.md). The
# predicate is recorded now so the bridge resolves the moment promotion lands, rather than being
# retrofitted later. Until then treat the object as an identifier, not a dereferenceable node.
#

@prefix smac:   <http://neuralamplifier.local/ontology/smac/> .
@prefix mech:   <http://neuralamplifier.local/ontology/smac/mechanic/> .
@prefix src:    <http://neuralamplifier.local/ontology/smac/source/> .
@prefix bobbin: <http://aegis.gastown.local/ontology/> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

src:thinker-neural-cpp a smac:Source ;
    rdfs:label "thinker src/neural.cpp" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Neural Amplifier adapter inside the Thinker fork." .

src:smac-player-ui a smac:Source ;
    rdfs:label "SMAC player interface" ;
    rdfs:comment "Stock behaviour available to any human player; not engine-specific." .

# --- How orders are offered -------------------------------------------------------------

mech:unit-activation-cycle a smac:TurnMechanic ;
    rdfs:label "Units awaiting orders are offered in a cycle" ;
    rdfs:comment """During a faction's turn the engine cycles through units that still have
movement, presenting each in turn for orders. The cycle is the DEFAULT path, not the only one:
it is an offer, and skipping or ignoring it does not forfeit the order.""" ;
    smac:appliesToEngine "smac" ;
    smac:ruleTier "canonical" ;
    smac:sourcedFrom src:smac-player-ui .

mech:direct-unit-addressing a smac:TurnMechanic ;
    rdfs:label "Any unit or base can be commanded directly, out of cycle" ;
    rdfs:comment """A player may select any owned unit or base at any point in their turn and
issue orders to it, without waiting for the activation cycle to reach it. This is what makes
DEPENDENT MOVES possible: a chain whose second step depends on the first can be ordered in that
order, rather than in whatever order the engine happens to offer.

Operationally this is the most important mechanic on this list for an agent, because the
one-at-a-time decision hook makes the cycle look mandatory when it is not.""" ;
    smac:appliesToEngine "smac" ;
    smac:ruleTier "canonical" ;
    smac:sourcedFrom src:smac-player-ui ;
    smac:enables mech:dependent-move-chaining .

mech:dependent-move-chaining a smac:TurnMechanic ;
    rdfs:label "Dependent moves can be chained within one turn" ;
    rdfs:comment """Because units can be addressed directly and in any order, a move whose
sensible choice depends on another unit having already moved can simply be ordered second. No
deferral mechanism is required; the ordering is the agent's to choose.""" ;
    smac:appliesToEngine "smac" ;
    smac:ruleTier "canonical" ;
    smac:sourcedFrom src:smac-player-ui .

# --- How production choices are applied -------------------------------------------------

mech:base-build-reasked-per-turn a smac:TurnMechanic ;
    rdfs:label "A base's production is asked several times per turn; the last answer wins" ;
    rdfs:comment """mod_base_reset is hooked at eleven call sites and each runs mod_base_build
followed by mod_base_change, so every call APPLIES its own answer and the last one to run is
what the base actually builds.

Measured in real play: 21 of 24 base-turns fired twice, 11 of those pairs disagreeing on the
choice. Three of the 24 fired only ONCE — so a plan that relies on getting a second chance at a
given base is relying on something that does not always happen.""" ;
    smac:appliesToEngine "thinker" ;
    smac:ruleTier "engine-observed" ;
    smac:sourcedFrom src:thinker-neural-cpp ;
    smac:computedBy bobbin:thinker.mod_base_build .

mech:build-queue-revisable a smac:TurnMechanic ;
    rdfs:label "A base's production item can be changed before it completes" ;
    rdfs:comment """Production is a queue setting rather than a committed act: accumulated
minerals stay with the base and the item may be changed on a later turn. Changing between
categories can incur the engine's retooling penalty, so revisable does not mean free.""" ;
    smac:appliesToEngine "smac" ;
    smac:ruleTier "canonical" ;
    smac:sourcedFrom src:smac-player-ui ;
    smac:reversible true .

mech:hurry-irreversible a smac:TurnMechanic ;
    rdfs:label "Hurrying production spends energy credits irreversibly" ;
    rdfs:comment """Hurrying deducts energy credits at the moment it applies and there is no
refund, no undo, and no later turn in which the decision can be revisited. It is the one
instrumented surface that spends something unrecoverable, which is why na.toml singles it out
as the first toggle to reach for if a run starts burning reserves.

Consequence for a planning agent: an irreversible action should be ordered LAST in a turn,
after the reversible ones have settled, because every earlier choice can still be revised in
light of it but not the other way round.""" ;
    smac:appliesToEngine "smac" ;
    smac:ruleTier "canonical" ;
    smac:sourcedFrom src:smac-player-ui ;
    smac:reversible false .

# --- What the engine does with an answer ------------------------------------------------

mech:engine-is-authoritative a smac:TurnMechanic ;
    rdfs:label "The engine may silently decline an order it accepted" ;
    rdfs:comment """An order can pass every availability test and still not survive: a later
engine path may overwrite the base's queue, a retool interaction may intervene, or a rule nobody
encoded may apply. The adapter therefore reads state back AFTER the apply and records a
divergence event when what the base holds differs from what was decided.

The mechanic to carry into planning: an accepted order is not an applied one, and only a
read-back distinguishes them.""" ;
    smac:appliesToEngine "thinker" ;
    smac:ruleTier "engine-observed" ;
    smac:sourcedFrom src:thinker-neural-cpp ;
    smac:computedBy bobbin:thinker.na_verify_base_production .
